ot for his wrestling. He came to Montréal with no international record, having never competed at the Worlds or Europeans, but he left a gold medalist. Petković was helped by a weak field with no major favorites. In round three he faced Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/60543"} "Vladimir Cheboksarov"] " in a desperately close match which ended in a 6-6 tie, but Petković was declared the winner on criteria, or various tie-breaking methods. Petković won all six of his matches, winning by disqualification in round one, and then taking six straight decisions to win the gold medal. He would later win silver medals at the World Championships in 1978-79 and 1981, but never again won a major international. Cheboksarov won silver while the bronze went to Bulgarian " [:a {:href "/athletes/57426"} "Ivan Kolev"] ", who lost his final match to Petković."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Round Eliminated"] [:th {} "Bad Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "361"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/60878"} "Momir Petković"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/YUG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/YUG.png"}] "YUG"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "322"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/60543"} "Vladimir Cheboksarov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "22"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/57426"} "Ivan Kolev"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/co